<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Force Publique</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Force Publique was the army and police force of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> and the Belgian Congo, created in 1885–1886, with African soldiers under European officers. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> it enforced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> quotas through hostage-taking, floggings and mutilations. It fought in both world wars, and its mutiny in July 1960 opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-crisis">Congo Crisis</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/force-publique.jpg" alt="Force Publique" width="320" height="286" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Force_Publique1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-of-belgium">Leopold II of Belgium</a>, who held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> as his private colony, ordered the Belgian Secretary of the Interior in 1885 to create a military for it. Early in 1886 Captain Léon Roget of the Carabiniers was sent to found the force and on 17 August became its commandant. Its officers were entirely European: Belgian regulars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, many from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. The African soldiers were recruited or conscripted in the Upper Congo or hired from Zanzibar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, until foreign mercenaries were mostly phased out by 1908.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-arab-war">Congo Arab war</a> of 1892–1894 the force defeated the African and Arab slavers, at a cost of tens of thousands of casualties, and in 1896 it beat the Rwandan royal army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shangi">Battle of Shangi</a> before withdrawing. It numbered 19,000 men by 1900. After the Belgian state took over the colony in 1908 the force was slowly reorganised. In the First World War Lieutenant-General Charles Tombeur's army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigali">Kigali</a> on 6 May 1916 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a> on 19 September 1916, supported by more than 260,000 local bearers.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 28 May 1940, Governor-General Pierre Ryckmans kept the colony in the war on the Allied side. The force grew to 40,000 men, fought Italian forces in Abyssinia, which surrendered to General Auguste-Édouard Gilliaert on 7 July 1941, and sent a medical unit as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Most of the force served in small fixed garrisons with a police role. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> kept it apart from the people it policed, in tribally mixed units with no more than a quarter of a company from its own province. The chicote was abolished only in 1955, and only about 20 African officer cadets were in training at independence. On the eve of independence it had 22,403 Congolese soldiers and NCOs under 444 European officers. After the mutiny it was renamed the Congolese National Army on 8 July 1960 and its leadership was Africanised; its former sergeant-major Joseph Mobutu later seized power.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Force Publique” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Free State’s instrument of power was the <b>Force Publique</b>, created in 1885–1886 as an army and police force of African soldiers under European officers, many of them Belgian, Scandinavian or Italian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Within weeks the <b>Force Publique</b> mutinied and Katanga and South Kasai broke away.</blockquote><a href="/state/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></li></ul></section>
